## Architecture North Star
This project should evolve toward a release-ready, modular, featurefirst Flutter architecture with:
- clear functional boundaries
- real module ownership per feature
- strong separation of concerns
- Clean Architecture-inspired layering 
- Cubit-based presentation state management (like Felix Angelov himself would implement the Cubit and the testing) 
- offline/persistence/sync treated as first-class architecture concerns 
- strong testing + CI/CD discipline (Code Magic) 
- long term maintainability over short term convenience 

The system should be easy to:
- navigate
- reason about
- test in isolation
- release safely
- scale across features and teams. 
- no comments needed, only if it's really necessary

## Project Philosophy 
1. Feature-First over Layer-First
Organize code primarily by feature not by technical type across the whole app. 
Why:
- reduces codebase jumping
- kepps related code close together
- supports stronger feature ownership
- improves onboarding and change isolation
- scales better for product development 

within each feature, keep layer separation explicit. 
- feature first at top level
- layered inside each fueature. 

2. Clean Code boundaries:
Each feature should have explicit boundaries between:
- data
- domain
- presentation 
i want use use cases in this Projekt. 

This is not ceremony. It is there to protect.
Maintainability, testability, long-termm product evolution, replacement of infrastructure details without touching ui. 

3. Cubits in Presentation
State management in the presentation layer uses cubit by default. 
Bloc may be used only when event driven complexity truly requires. 
The Project standard is ui renders state, Cubit orchestrates presentation flow, domain contains business rules and use cases, data handles transport, persistence, mapping, and sync details. 

Engineering Benchmarks:
The project should be designed with an engineering mindset closer to serious product codebases. 
- boundaries matter, reliability matters, ci cd matters, testability matters, offline peristence stratefy masters, releases must be safe and repeatable. 

Target folder Structure:
- lib
- core/error,network, config, logging, analytics, monitoring, feature_flags, local_storage, testing, desgn_system 
- feature/
auth/data/datasources, models, dtos, mappers, repositories
domain/entities, value_objects, repositories, usecases
Presentation/cubit/screens/widgets
app/app.dart router.dart di.dart bootstrap.dart

ui: 
- must not know transport details
- must not parsew raw api payloads
- must not contain persistence logic
- must not contain long-lived business rules
- should depend on domain contracts/use cases not on low level data tetails directly
- must remain easy to widget test

Cubit:
- one cubit = one primary presentation responsibility
- methos should represent user intent 
- states should be immutable
- status enum should be the single source of truth for lifecycle when applicable
- cubit handles presentation flow, not infrastructure details. 

Domain Layer:
Contains:
- entities, value objects, repository contracts, use cases, business rules
Rules: 
- keep flutter dependencides out if possible
- model business language not api language
- be stable and highly testable
- avoid leaking dto or storage structure into the domain

Use Case Policy:
Use Cases are expected in this architecture. 
However, the must be meaningful. 
Do not create ceremonial use cases that simply forward one repository call without any architectural value unless the codebase already standardizes even simple flows that way. 

Use a use case when: 
- it captures a business action
- it coordinates logic
- it improves readability
- it improves testability
- it creates a stable application-level boundary. 

Data Layer:
Contains:
- DTos / models,
- remote data sources
- local data sources
- sync adapters
- mappers
- repository implementations

Rules: 
- all infrastructure lives here
- transport/storage specifics stay here
- repository implementation translate between domain and infrastructure
- logging and infrastructure-level error capturte belongs here
- local persistence and remote sync policy must be explicit here. 

Data Layer Expectations:
The data layer must be designed for:
- unreliable networks
- retries where appropriate
- persistence strategy clarity
- cache invalidation strategy clarity
- deterministic mapping
- safe deserialization behavior 

---

Core Modules Responsibilites:
The core/ folder is for shared cross cutting capabilities not random dumping!
Every subfolder must have a narrow purpose. 
core/error:
- shared error types, failure Modeling, error translation primitives, app-wide error contracts where justified

core/network: 
- http client wrappers, request building helpers, connectivity-aware abstractions if the repo uses them, shared network policies (timeout, headers, auth attachment)

core/config:
- enviroment config
- build flavor config
- app constants tied to enviroment/runtime

core/logging:
- structured logging abstractions
- logger setup
- logging sinks/adapters if needed

core/analytrics
- event tracking contracts
- analytics adapers
- event naming standards

core/monitoring
- crash/error monitoring integration
- non-functional observability hooks

core/feature_flags
local/remote flags
- rollout helpers
- kill switch support if applicable

core/local_storage/
- shared persistence helpers, secure/löocal storage adaperts, storage serialization conventions

core/testing:
test fixtures, shareed mocks/fakes, test builders, helpers for feature/module tests

core/design_systems:
- reusable ui primitives, tokens, spacing, typography, color rules, standardized error/loading/empty/retry components

Core Rules:
- core must not become a dumping around
- if code is used by only one feature, its belongs in that feature
- shared code must be shared because it is truly cross-cutting not because someone wanted a utils folder. 

Real module System rules: 
treat every feature as a real module with ownership and Boundaries. 

Feature Boundary Rules: 
A feature may expose:
- presentation entry points as needed by routing
- domain contracts/(entities/use cases if other modules legitimately depend on them
- carefully selected shared widgets only if the are truly reusable and belongs in a shared space 

A feature must not freely reach into another featue's internals. 

Forbidding Coupling:
Do not: 
- import another features internal data layer directly
- reuse antoher feature private dtos models
- depend on another feature Cubit unless explicitly designed as shared presentation logic
- use another feature as an unofficial utility bucket. 

Allowed Cross Feature Interactions:
If features need to interact do it through:
- domain contracts, 
- app level routing/navigation contracts
- shared core abstractions
- explicit integration points. 
==========

Dependency Direction: 
use this dependency flow as the default rule:
- presentation => domain
- data => domain
- app => Features/core
- core should stay as indendent and stable as possible

Avoid!!
- domain => presentation
- domain => data implementation
- feature A => Feature B internal Layers. 

When a dependency must cross boundaries, prefer explicit contracts. 
============
Offline / Persistence / Sync Policy
Offline behavior is an architectural decision, not an afterthought. 

Every Feature that handles important user data should answer these questions explicitly:
- What is stored locally?
- what is cached temporarily
- what is source of truth?
- what happens when network is unavailable?
- is the feature offlien-readable?
- is the feature offline-writable?
- is there queued sync?
- how are conflicts resolved?
- what invalidates cached/local data?

Persistence Modes:
Each feature should consciously choose one of these modes:
- remote first
- Cache assisted
- offline-readable
- offline-readable ü offline writable with sync

do not let this happen accidentally. 

===========
Sync Rules:
if sync exists define:
sync trigger points, retry behavior, conflict policy, merge stratgy, iddempotency expectations, failure visibility to user. 

Storage Rules:
- local storage format should be explicit
- mapping between local models and domain should be deterministic
- do not hide persistence side effects in UI. 
===========
Release Readiness Standard:

the project should be built with release discipline from the start. 
A Feature is not done unless it is:
- architecturally placed corrrectly 
- localized
- test-covered at the correct level
- observable/loggable where relevant
- safe under error conditions
- consistent with navigation/state/error/loading conventions
- ready for ci validation

Release ready expectations:
- deterministic build pipeline
- analuzzable and testable in CI
- enviroment config separated cleanly
- feature flags or safe rollout strategy where needed
- failure minitoring integrated
- no hidden debug-only assumptions in prodction code. 

Test Strategy:
Testing is a first class architecture convern. 

Test Pyramid: 
Prefer a balanced mix of: 
- unit tests: for domain/use cases/entities/value objects/mappers
- cubit tests: for state flow
- repoitory tests: for mapping + error behavior
- widget tests: for screen behavior and state rendering 
- integration tests for critical user Flows. 

What must be easy to test:
- domain rules
- use causes
- input normalization
- mapping logic
- repository success/failure behavior
- cubit lifecycle and transitions
- offline/cache behavior where applicable

Testing Rules:
- test behavior not implementation trivia
- use fakers/buolders/fixtures from core/testing/ when helpful
- avoid fragile tests tied to privatze implementations details
- keep tests readable and modular. 

CI/CD Standards
Ci/CD is part of the architecture. 
The repository should support safe, repeatable delivery. 
Minimum CI expectations:
every chgange should be able to run. 
bash flutter analyze flutter test. 

And where applicable: 
- formatting validation - integration tests - coverage reporting - flavor/buold validation - artifact generation

Strong CI/CD Direction:
Prefer a pipeline that includes:
- static analysis
- unit/widget/integration test stages
- build verification per target/flavor
- release artificat validation
- changelog/version discipline if used by the team
- optional gated merges for protected branches

Branch/ Merge quality Expectations:
- changes should be revieable in small prs
- every pr should be scoped and intentional
- feature work should not sneak in unrelated refactos
- ci failures block merge readiness
==============
Build / bootstrap / App Composition
The app folder should remain thin and compositional

app/bootstrap.dart
 - app startup sequence
- environment/bootstrap wiring
- top-level initialization

app/di.dart
- dependency registration
- module wiring
- feature registration if needed

app/router.dart
- route table /navigation composition
- guard wiring if needed

app/app.dart
- root app widget
- theme, l10n, router integration

RUles:
- keep app compositione xplicit
- avoid hidden bootstrap side effects
- avoid mixing feature logic into top-level app wiring 

Design System Should own:
- spacing scale
- typography scale
- colors/tokens/themes
- common buttons/fields/cardss/surfaces
- loading/empty/error/retry states
- accessibility-minded reusable widgets. 

Rules:
- prefer design system components over ad-hoc ui duplication
- repeated loading and error patterns hsould converge into reusable primitives
- feature widgets should compose from the design system where practical 

Feature Flags / Safe Rollout
 Feature Flags are an engineering tool, not a dumping ground. 
Use them when they help with:
- staged rollout
- kill switches
- enviroment-specific enablement
- high risk feature isolation

Rules:
- feature flag ownership must be clear
- stale flags should be removed
- flags should not permanently replace clean architecture decisions 

Anti-patterns to avoid:
Avoid the following:
- layer-first global sprawl across the whole project
- feature internals imported by other features
- utils dumping grounds
- DTOs leaking into ui/domain
- no offline/persistence stragy for data-heavy features
- new abstractions without team/product value
- cubits with infrastructure/persistence converns embedded in them
- repository methods without consistens logging/error behavior
- shared code that is not actually shared
- app-level files turning into a second business layer
============
Final rule: 
this projectshould feel like a serious product codebase not a collection of screens:
That meanse:
- clear module ownership
- explicit boundaries
- disciplined layering
- reliable testability
- meaningful CI/CD
- offline/persistence decisions made consciously
- release-readiness treated as a feature, not a finishing touch. 

One line Summary:
Build this repository as a feature-first, cubit-driven, clean-architecture-inspired, release-ready Flutter product with real module boundaries, strong ci/testing discipline, offline/persistence awareness and long-term maintainability as a first class requirement.


